Subject: [PATCH] webgpu: preferred canvas format from the claimed platform

navigator.gpu.getPreferredCanvasFormat() is decided by the platform the binary
was built for:

  #if BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID) || BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
    return wgpu::TextureFormat::RGBA8Unorm;
  #else
    return wgpu::TextureFormat::BGRA8Unorm;
  #endif

There are exactly two legitimate values and they partition the platforms, so
rgba8unorm means Android or Linux and bgra8unorm means everything else. A Linux
build serving a macOS or Windows profile therefore contradicts its own user
agent, navigator.platform and Client Hints on a value a page reads in one call.

That is a worse class of defect than a wrong number. Ruling on it needs no
reference data and no per-version table — only the platform the client itself
claims — because there is no configuration in which a macOS Chrome returns
rgba8unorm.

It is also not an isolated bug but the second instance of a family. The first is
WebBluetooth in runtime_enabled_features.json5, fixed in patch 0017. Both are
web-facing values resolved by the preprocessor from the build platform, so no
amount of work at the JavaScript layer reaches either. The third instance,
BarcodeDetector, is deliberately left unfixed because its backing service has no
Linux implementation and exposing it would swap a missing API for a broken one.

The condition here is reproduced rather than replaced: profiles claiming Android
or Linux still get RGBA8Unorm, exactly as upstream gives them. Only the input
changes, from the build platform to the claimed one. With no profile loaded the
original #if is untouched.

On the upstream comment, which attributes the Linux branch to mesa BGRA driver
bugs under Vulkan/GL interop: that is a property of the host's driver stack, not
of the device being claimed, and it is a rendering concern rather than an
identity one. A profile claiming macOS must report bgra8unorm or it is not that
machine. If the host's driver mishandles BGRA, the answer is a host or ANGLE
backend that does not, rather than emitting the wrong value.

Does not move the V3 score on its own: the webgpu probe also fails on adapter
absence, since there is no WebGPU adapter under SwiftShader on the build host.
That is a separate row and is not addressed here.

Ledger: webgpu.preferred-canvas-format.
diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu.cc b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu.cc
index 2052ca5..8051e2b 100644
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu.cc
@@ -4,6 +4,8 @@
 
 #include "third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu.h"
 
+#include "base/apostate/profile.h"
+
 #include <utility>
 
 #include "base/feature_list.h"
@@ -417,6 +419,33 @@ V8GPUTextureFormat GPU::getPreferredCanvasFormat() {
 }
 
 wgpu::TextureFormat GPU::GetPreferredCanvasFormat() {
+  // Upstream decides this by the platform the binary was *built* for, which
+  // makes it a compile-time contradiction of any profile claiming a different
+  // OS: rgba8unorm means Android or Linux and bgra8unorm means everything else,
+  // with no other legitimate values. A page comparing this against the platform
+  // in the user agent, navigator.platform and the Client Hints needs no
+  // reference data at all to rule on the disagreement.
+  //
+  // So it follows the claimed platform instead, reproducing upstream's own
+  // condition rather than inventing a mapping. Profiles that claim Android or
+  // Linux get exactly what upstream gives them.
+  //
+  // The upstream comment attributes the Linux branch to mesa BGRA driver bugs
+  // under Vulkan/GL interop. That is a property of the host's driver stack, not
+  // of the device being claimed, and it is a rendering-correctness concern
+  // rather than a correctness-of-identity one. A profile claiming macOS or
+  // Windows must report bgra8unorm or it is not that machine; if the host's
+  // driver then mishandles BGRA, that is a reason to choose a host or an ANGLE
+  // backend that does not, not a reason to emit the wrong value.
+  if (const base::apostate::Profile* profile = base::apostate::Profile::Get()) {
+    if (const std::optional<std::string>& platform = profile->ua_platform()) {
+      if (*platform == "Android" || *platform == "Linux") {
+        return wgpu::TextureFormat::RGBA8Unorm;
+      }
+      return wgpu::TextureFormat::BGRA8Unorm;
+    }
+  }
+
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID) || BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
   // Interop of vulkan and GL has mesa driver bugs for BGRA format
   // See anglebug.com/40644739
